**Item 1 — Haynes/Moody lab, Duke (bioRxiv 10.64898/2026.07.16.738984, posted 2026-07-21)**
A conventional monovalent H1 hemagglutinin trimer is sufficient to elicit stem-directed antibodies in rhesus macaques — competitive blocking of CR6261 in all animals, DH726.1 protects against lethal H1N1 + H5N1 challenge, cryo-EM confirms stem footprint. The finding resets the baseline for what conventional HA vaccination can achieve when measured with the right assay.

**Item 2 — Pillai lab, MGH (bioRxiv 10.64898/2026.07.15.738751, posted 2026-07-21)**
Germinal-center-deficient mice generate a durable, class-switched memory B cell population through a T-cell-dependent but GC-independent route. These cells show greater repertoire diversity than canonical GC-derived memory and protect against vaccine-matched AND unmatched influenza strains — identifying a GC-independent path to broadly cross-reactive influenza immunity.

Both are abstract-only (bioRxiv body not yet rendered); this is noted in the script. The episode runs in SKIP_TTS mode (consistent with the other 3 episodes today), producing the script `.md`, RSS item stub `.rss-item.xml`, and commit message `.commit-msg` for `publish_pending.py` to handle after batch TTS.
